    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Using NSAIDs like ibuprofen can help manage pain and inflammation, however, they should not be relied upon as a primary mode of treatment, especially when considering the impact of any potential activities that might aggravate your condition. A sharp back pain combined with a 'shooting' sensation down your leg suggests a neurological issue that might not respond well to self-treatment strategies, especially those involving intense twists or stagnate alignment attempts. \n\nWhile an intense yoga twist can sometimes aid in stretching a muscle rather than a vertebrate structure (since a genuine spinal twist in a posterior position is stretching for intervertebral discs, which could risk exacerbation of a herniated disc), it's important to first ensure that any movement you make is safe with your current state of pain and discomfort. Start with very gentle movements only, ideally under the guidance of a qualified professional who understands the nuances of your condition. They can instruct safer stretches that provide therapeutic benefit without undue risk.\n\nIf newly-introduced intense movement isn't recommended, consider gentle stretches that do not simulate a twisted pose and instead focus on mobility and relaxation, like gentle forward bends, cat-cow stretches, and pelvic tilts.\n\n\nIn terms of managing the pain, rest, ice, keeping the affected area elevated,",
